Done. All 6 tests pass with pristine output.

**`src/validate-email.js`** (new):

```js
function isValidEmail(value) {
  if (typeof value !== 'string') {
    return false;
  }

  const atIndex = value.indexOf('@');
  if (atIndex < 1) {
    return false;
  }

  return value.slice(atIndex + 1).includes('.');
}
```

**`test/validate-email.test.js`** (new) — 6 tests, and **`package.json`** now has `"test": "node --test"`.

Built with the TDD cycle, five red-green rounds, each test watched failing before the code that passes it:

| Test | Failure observed first | Code it drove |
|---|---|---|
| accepts `user@example.com` | `false !== true` | initial function |
| rejects `user.example.com` | `true !== false` | `@` check |
| rejects `@example.com` | `true !== false` | `atIndex > 0` |
| rejects `user@example`, `first.last@example` | `true !== false` | dot searched only *after* the @ |
| rejects `undefined`/`null`/`42` | `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined` | type guard |

Two notes:

- **Test framework:** the project had none and zero dependencies, so I used Node's built-in `node:test` runner rather than pulling in Jest. Say the word if you'd prefer a different one.
- **Scope:** this is a structural check, not RFC 5322 conformance — it accepts things like `a@b.c` and `us er@ex ample.com` since you didn't specify rules about whitespace, length, or trailing dots. Easy to add if you want them.
